 ## Context
 
-Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
+Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest. The kiosk has no network route during operation, and the HLD requires every lookup to complete using files on the kiosk.
 
 ## Architecture
 
 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a **local embedded SQLite index**: each kiosk maintains its own SQLite database file, built locally when a snapshot arrives. There is no lookup service to run and no network dependency; the entire registry is one database file plus the incoming snapshot. The certificates node sees none of this — its interface and behavior are unchanged by the backend choice.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness.
+
+Import builds a fresh SQLite database beside the active one: parse the snapshot records, load them into the new database file with a unique index on the normalized serial column, and record the snapshot date in the database. The active snapshot is never written in place. On success, activate the new database by an atomic rename over a fixed active-database path. A failed import deletes the partial database and leaves the previous snapshot active and serving lookups throughout, and reports a useful error. Each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives; there is no shared or centrally maintained index.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Normalization is applied both when records are loaded into the index (stored in the normalized-serial column) and when a lookup serial is queried, so a technician-typed serial matches the loaded record set exactly.
+
+At lookup time the registry opens the active SQLite database read-only, queries the normalized-serial column through its unique index, and returns the record plus the snapshot date stored in that database. If the active database file is missing or unreadable, the registry reports a lookup failure rather than returning a fabricated NotFound; the caller cannot distinguish "no such serial" from "registry unavailable" by result shape alone. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index per kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | The HLD tenet requires lookups to complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access; both networked options violate that constraint. Among file-local choices, SQLite gives predictable keyed lookups on 90,000 records, no service to operate, and an atomic single-file swap for snapshot activation. The cost — each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index per snapshot — stays inside this node and runs at import time, not lookup time. |
+| Snapshot activation | Atomic rename of a fully built database over a fixed active path | In-place update of the active database; dual-database selection flag | In-place update would expose partially imported records on a crash; the fixed-path rename keeps the "previous snapshot survives a failed import" invariant with a single file operation. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index — selected among the three sketched options; the HLD offline tenet ruled out both networked options.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. SQLite database file location on the kiosk filesystem (implementation-time choice; only the fixed active-path swap is fixed by this design).
+2. Concurrent access behavior if a snapshot import runs while a technician performs a lookup (single-technician kiosks make this rare; revisit during implementation).
+3. Retention policy for superseded snapshot databases (currently assumed: delete after successful activation).
